llback in detectSetPoint when no bent-elbow frames found iterates again with redundant initialization","suggestion":"The minWristY variable is re-initialized to Infinity inside the fallback block (line 867-873), which is correct but could be simplified by combining the loops or using a different approach."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/keyframe-detector.ts","line":191,"issue":"calculateKneeAngle has duplicate logic that could use calculateJointAngle","suggestion":"The calculateKneeAngle function duplicates the angle calculation logic from calculateJointAngle. Consider refactoring to: `return calculateJointAngle(hip, knee, ankle);` for consistency with calculateElbowAngle and calculateWristAngle."}],"summary":"The implementation correctly fulfills all acceptance criteria for Set Point and Release Detection (Task 2.3). The code implements:\n\n1. **detectSetPoint()**: Finds the frame with highest wrist position (minimum Y in normalized coords) while elbow is still bent (angle < threshold). Falls back to just using highest wrist position if no bent-elbow frames are found.\n\n2. **detectRelease()**: Finds the frame with maximum wrist flexion (minimum wrist angle = maximum snap) after the set point. The search explicitly starts from `setPointFrame + 1` ensuring release always occurs after set_point.\n\n3. **Edge cases handled**:\n   - Quick release with minimal pause: Both keyframes detected even when 2 frames apart (test at line 1636)\n   - Temporal ordering enforced: release only searched after set_point (line 898-899)\n   - Pump fake/hesitation: The algorithm finds the final highest point before release by design\n   - Jump shot vs set shot: Uses both wrist Y position and elbow angle to distinguish\n\n4. **Additional helper functions**: calculateElbowAngle() and calculateWristAngle() are properly implemented using the shared calculateJointAngle() logic.\n\n5. **Tests**: 89 tests pass including comprehensive coverage of the new functionality with edge cases. The test